  "worked_solution": "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \u27e8\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,\\,2{e}^{(2t+1)}\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t=\u27e8\\int \\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 2{e}^{(2t+1)}\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x(t)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t+{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t)=2{e}^{(2t+1)},$ we calculate its integral using substitution. So, we let $u=2t+1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 2{e}^{(2t+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 3{e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{u}+{c}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{(2t+1)}}{2}+{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \u27e8\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,\\,3{e}^{(2t+1)}\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t & =\u27e8\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t+{c}_{1},\\,\\frac{3{e}^{(2t+1)}}{2}+{c}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t,\\,\\frac{3{e}^{(2t+1)}}{2}\u27e9+\u27e8{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t,\\,\\frac{3{e}^{(2t+1)}}{2}\u27e9+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
